```
ROLE: product_realist  VOTE: yellow
REAL_USE_CASE: Teams pretraining on short-sequence corpora (MAXLEN≤128) with low docs-per-pack (~2.0) and long-tail length distributions where domain correlates with length; NOT standard production pretraining with long contexts (≥512) that pack dense (dpp≥5.8).
OPERATIONAL_SIGNIFICANCE: Microbenchmark-only for production: ratio decays monotonically 1.22→1.17→1.10 as docs-per-pack 2.0→3.1→5.8, with 1.10 CI[0.772,1.545] including 1.0 at production-relevant density (packet L0 RESULTS table). Real but confined to low-dpp regime; at dense packing the tax is statistically indistinguishable from zero.
ADOPTION_BARRIER: [Effect size below operational threshold at production densities (1.10, CI includes 1.0); Length-sorted packing is throughput-optimal (minimizes padding waste) — shuffled packing would increase waste and hurt TCO; Frozen tiny analytic-CE model may not transfer to mid-training real-model dynamics (regime-dependence flagged in packet); No downstream metric validation (loss/perplexity/steps-to-target) — GNS is proxy only; Within-pack averaging already mitigates the issue in dense regimes, making intervention redundant]
RATIONALE: Packet L0 RESULTS shows monotone decay with docs-per-pack: "ratio 1.22 -> 1.17 -> 1.10 as docs/pack 2.0 -> 3.1 -> 5.8" and "At low averaging (MAXLEN=128, dpp=2.0, realistic 10k buffer) the effect is real and significant (ratio 1.22, CI excludes 1.0). As pack density rises, within-pack averaging washes out the domain homogeneity and the ratio decays toward 1.0 — exactly the predicted strongest null exit (B)." Packet THREE THINGS TO STRESS-TEST (a) explicitly questions "is a BOUNDED 1.10-1.22x ... confined to the LOW-docs-per-pack regime ... too small/regime-narrow to matter (production pretraining often packs DENSE)?" The negative result is actionable: for dense packing (dpp>5), the length-sort tax is negligible; for low-dpp short-seq regimes, consider domain-interleaving or increase pack density. Citation: Krell 2107.02027 'without impacting performance' covers attention cross-contamination only, not batch-composition (packet ORCHESTRATOR NOTE).
```
